1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,366
Minimum payment due: $150.

2
00:00:03,786 --> 00:00:05,790
It looks manageable. That's the point.

3
00:00:06,210 --> 00:00:14,225
Here's a $5,000 balance at 24%. Many cards set the minimum at 1% of the balance, plus that month's interest.

4
00:00:14,645 --> 00:00:20,955
So the first minimum is $150. $100 of it is interest. Only $50 touches the balance.

5
00:00:21,725 --> 00:00:24,100
Here's why that number keeps you paying for years.

6
00:00:24,870 --> 00:00:27,060
The trap. The minimum shrinks as you pay.

7
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:33,218
A year in, it's down to $134. By year five, $83. By year ten, $45.

8
00:00:33,638 --> 00:00:37,956
It feels like progress. But a smaller payment means the balance barely moves.

9
00:00:38,376 --> 00:00:46,908
Paying only the minimum takes 201 months. Almost 17 years. And $8,442 in interest. More than the original balance.

10
00:00:47,678 --> 00:00:49,767
The fix is simple. Freeze the payment.

11
00:00:50,187 --> 00:00:52,999
Paying the shrinking minimum: two hundred and one months.

12
00:00:53,419 --> 00:01:02,293
Keep paying the first minimum, $150, every month, even as the minimum drops: 56 months, and $3,322 in interest.

13
00:01:02,713 --> 00:01:07,172
Pay $250, and it's gone in 26 months, for $1,449.

14
00:01:07,592 --> 00:01:17,807
And here's something most people skip. In the US, your card statement has a box showing how long minimum payments will take, and the monthly payment that clears it in three years.

15
00:01:18,227 --> 00:01:22,173
For this card, that's about $197 a month. Gone in three years.

16
00:01:22,593 --> 00:01:23,456
So, this week.

17
00:01:23,616 --> 00:01:25,957
Find the minimum-payment box on your statement.

18
00:01:26,117 --> 00:01:28,511
Freeze your payment at today's minimum, or more.

19
00:01:28,671 --> 00:01:31,377
Set autopay to that fixed amount, not the minimum.

20
00:01:31,537 --> 00:01:33,795
And send raises and windfalls straight at it.

21
00:01:34,565 --> 00:01:41,360
Minimum formulas vary by card, so check yours. The Paycheck Planner in the description shows your real payoff date.

22
00:01:41,780 --> 00:01:44,535
Subscribe for more money math, with real numbers.
